# One robot, two brains

Topic 14

After this talk you can:

- explain how a gate chooses between two sets of subsystems
- say what "fail closed" means and where Hadur does it
- describe minimum-risk movement and a field gun in a sentence each

---

## Two games, one robot

A duel (one opponent) and a free-for-all (up to nine) need different play.

Duel: lock the radar, surf one enemy's waves, learn its profile.

Melee: sweep the radar, keep away from crowds, shoot whoever is cheapest to kill.

Hadur is a duelist first. The duel was measured, tuned and ranked 20th of 1,216. Melee must not be allowed to damage it.

---

## "1v1 is sacred"

The melee work added packages and routed around the duel. It never edited it.

A test enforces that. It hashes every source file in the duel's packages and compares with a stored snapshot.

```java
static final List<String> DUEL_PACKAGES = List.of(
    "adapt", "gun", "knn", "ledger", "memory",
    "move", "physics", "policy", "shield");
```

(`DuelIdentityTest`.) Add, edit or remove a duel file and the build fails with the name of the file. A duel stage re-pins deliberately, with `-Dhadur.duel.snapshot=write`.

---

## The gate

Two postures, `DUEL` and `MELEE`, never mixed in one tick. One set of subsystems drives and produces the orders.

```java
public Posture evaluate(int others, int numSentries) {
    return others >= 2 && numSentries == 0 && veto == Veto.NONE
        ? Posture.MELEE : Posture.DUEL;
}
```

Melee needs all of: two or more opponents, no sentry alive, and no veto this round. Anything else is the duel.

That shape is "fail closed". The default answer is the safe one. The risky one needs every condition to hold.

---

## Vetoes

A veto lasts for the rest of the round (GATE-3, GATE-4).

```java
public void sentryScanned(String name) {
    if (sentries.size() < MAX_SENTRIES) sentries.add(name);
    if (veto == Veto.NONE) veto = Veto.SENTRY;
}
public void meleeFailed() { veto = Veto.FAULT; }
```

- A sentry robot (a guard on the field's border) vetoes melee. Its name is remembered for the whole battle (GATE-5).
- A fault in the melee code vetoes melee. A sentry seen after a fault does not hide the fault.
- The set of sentry names is capped at 32 (RES-2).

---

## Catch, record, hand over

If the melee code throws, the core catches it and gives that same tick to the duel.

```java
try {
    meleeTick(in, orders);
} catch (RuntimeException ex) {
    // GATE-4: fail closed. The duel takes this tick
    // and the rest of the round.
    orders = meleeFailed(in, ex);
}
```

`meleeFailed` records the fault, tells the gate, resets the duel's tracking and returns fresh orders. The robot never stalls. In the M6 melee benches: 0 melee faults.

---

## Strategy pattern, and what Hadur actually does

The textbook pattern: an interface, two implementations, a context that holds one.

```java
interface Brain { BotOrders tick(BotInput in); }
// Brain duel = ...; Brain melee = ...;
// Brain active = gate.evaluate(...) == MELEE ? melee : duel;
```

(A teaching sketch, not Hadur code.)

Hadur uses an enum and a branch in `HadurCore.tick` instead, because the two sets of subsystems share state (the tracker, the profile hand-off, the tick budget). The point is the same: choose one behaviour per tick, behind one decision, with a safe default.

---

## Minimum-risk movement

Each tick, score 160 points on rings around Hadur (32 angles by 5 rings). Go to the least risky.

```java
for (Point2D.Double p : candidates) {
    double r = risk(p, v);
    if (r < bestRisk) { bestRisk = r; best = p; }
}
if (destination != null && destination.distance(me) > REACHED
        && bestRisk >= SWITCH_GAIN * risk(destination, v)) {
    return destination;      // keep going unless a point is 10% safer
}
```

Risk: each opponent's energy over distance squared, doubled if Hadur would be its closest robot, plus virtual bullets from every recorded shot, plus walls, corners and the centre.

---

## What mattered (M6 sweep)

Reference field, 35 rounds by 10 seeds (docs/bench/m6-gates.md):

| Build | APS | Survival |
|---|---|---|
| baseline | 54.0 | 63.3 |
| no virtual bullets | 50.9 | 56.6 |
| bullet weight 2.0 | 51.8 | 58.5 |
| most other weights | 52.9 to 54.0 | 61.4 to 63.9 |

The weights sit at a local optimum. The one that matters is the virtual-bullet term: remove it and APS falls 3 points and survival 7.

---

## A gun that aims at everyone at once

The field gun gives each opponent scanned in the last 8 ticks three firing solutions, replayed from its history. Each solution covers its angle plus or minus the robot's half-width, weighted by distance, energy and recent behaviour.

```java
for (Solution o : all) {
    if (Math.abs(Angles.normalRelativeAngle(
            s.angle - o.angle)) <= o.tolerance) {
        density += o.weight;
    }
}
```

It fires where the summed density peaks. A shot that could hit two opponents beats a shot at one.

---

## The result

Cold, 1000 by 1000, from `docs/bench/m6-gates.md` and the README:

| Melee bench | 2.2 | 3.0 (M6) |
|---|---|---|
| Reference field (9 melee bots), APS | 37.9 | 52.7 to 54.6 |
| Reference, survival | 32.6 | 59.5 to 63.4 |
| Challenge field (9 sample bots), firsts of 100 | 33 | 72 |

The plan's gate, reference APS 60, was not met, and the report says so. Hadur is 28th in the MeleeRumble (Leigh, 2026-10-01), and the duel's Shadow share moved from 57.4% to 54.5% ± 4.5, inside the noise of both.

---

## Recap

- Choose behaviour in one place, with a safe default.
- Failing closed: the risky path needs every condition, and any fault hands over to the proven path.
- Protect the part that works with a test that cannot be argued with.
- Minimum risk is a loop over candidates and a function that scores them.
- Write down the gate you missed.

